---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getMeta } from '../utils/data';
const meta = getMeta();
---

<Base
  title="Does Harden Choke?"
  current="dhc"
  description="Does anything explain why some NBA stars have terrible playoff games more than others?"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h1 class="display">Does Harden Choke?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A 31-player study of why superstar scorers sometimes have terrible games in the playoffs.
      </p>

      <h2>The question</h2>
      <p>
        Does anything explain why some players have terrible games in the playoffs than other similar superstars?
        Is there a common trigger — pressure, elimination games, tough defenses — or is each player's playoff
        struggle its own story?
      </p>

      <h2>What we found</h2>
      <p>
        We didn't find a single trigger or pattern that explains the whole cohort. James Harden is a separate
        case — his playoff floor collapses have a specific structure that doesn't generalize to the other 30
        players we studied.
      </p>
      <p>
        What <em>does</em> generalize: players who lose free throws from the regular season to the postseason are
        more likely to have terrible games. When the whistle dries up, scoring floors drop. The relationship is
        strong across the cohort (r = {meta.headlines.harden_fta_correlation}, p = 0.002), though it's not
        universal — Jimmy Butler is a clear exception.
      </p>

      <h2>More detail</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Harden's elimination-game floor rate (14.6%) is actually <em>lower</em> than his non-elimination playoff
          rate (23.2%) — the "choker in big moments" framing doesn't hold for him on that axis.
        </li>
        <li>
          The "playoff whistle" isn't league-wide: the cohort's mean FTA shift from regular season to playoffs is
          −0.05 FTA/36 — essentially noise on average.
        </li>
        <li>
          Harden floors at high rates against both elite and weak defenses. Most stars in the cohort show a clear
          opponent-quality gradient; Harden doesn't.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Full write-up in the <a href="https://github.com/hirememorey/does-harden-choke">does-harden-choke repo</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
